ngterm-pty runs each Windows child tree in a job object with JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE, so ending a pane
ends its shell's descendants too. Assigning the job after CreateProcessW leaves a window in which the child can start
processes outside it, and portable-pty closes the main thread handle a suspended start would need. The child is put
into the job as it is created instead, through PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_JOB_LIST beside the pseudoconsole attribute
(Windows 10 and newer, as ConPTY itself): `ConPtyMasterPty::spawn_command_in_job`, since the slave behind
`PtyPair::slave` cannot be downcast. ngterm's own patch; remove when portable-pty takes a job for the spawn.

diff -ruN a/src/win/conpty.rs b/src/win/conpty.rs
--- a/src/win/conpty.rs
+++ b/src/win/conpty.rs
@@ -77,6 +77,21 @@
     inner: Arc<Mutex<Inner>>,
 }
 
+impl ConPtyMasterPty {
+    /// Spawn `cmd` attached to this pseudoconsole, assigned to the job object
+    /// `job` as it is created, so neither it nor any process it starts runs
+    /// outside the job. `job` must stay open until this returns.
+    pub fn spawn_command_in_job(
+        &self,
+        cmd: CommandBuilder,
+        job: std::os::windows::io::RawHandle,
+    ) -> anyhow::Result<Box<dyn Child + Send + Sync>> {
+        let inner = self.inner.lock().unwrap();
+        let child = inner.con.spawn_command_in_job(cmd, Some(job as _))?;
+        Ok(Box::new(child))
+    }
+}
+
 pub struct ConPtySlavePty {
     inner: Arc<Mutex<Inner>>,
 }
